      title: "\"Who Do You Say That I Am?\"",
      body: `## The central question

At Caesarea Philippi, Jesus asked his disciples, "Whom do men say that I am?" They reported various opinions: John the Baptist, Elijah, one of the prophets. Then he asked, "But whom say ye that I am?" Peter answered, "Thou art the Christ" (Mark 8:27–29). The question has been at the center of Christian theology ever since. The branch of theology that studies it is called **Christology**.

## Titles

The New Testament gives Jesus many titles, each with a background in the Jewish Scriptures or the Greco-Roman world.

- **Messiah** or **Christ**: the anointed king of David's line, expected to restore Israel. The title became so attached to Jesus that "Christ" functioned almost as a surname.
- **Son of Man**: Jesus's favorite way of referring to himself in the Gospels. In Hebrew and Aramaic it can simply mean "a human being," but in Daniel 7:13 "one like the Son of man" comes with the clouds of heaven and receives everlasting dominion. Scholars debate how Jesus used it.
- **Son of God**: used of Israel's king (Psalm 2:7) and of Israel itself (Hosea 11:1), and in the Roman world of the emperor. In the New Testament it comes to express a unique relationship with the Father.
- **Lord** (*kyrios*): the Septuagint's word for the divine name. As we saw in BIB 110, Paul applies to Jesus texts about the Lord God.
- **Word** (*logos*): in John 1, the eternal Word who was with God and was God, through whom all things were made, and who "was made flesh."

## Worship

As we saw in BIB 110, devotion to Jesus as divine appeared very early, among Jewish believers who were fiercely monotheistic. They prayed to him, sang hymns to him, baptized in his name, and confessed him as Lord. The question for later theology was how to understand this worship. If Jesus is worshipped, is he God? If he is God, how is there still one God? And if he is God, how is he also a human being who was born, grew, suffered, and died?

## From above and from below

Modern theologians sometimes distinguish two approaches to Christology.

- **Christology from above** begins with the eternal Son of God, who became incarnate. This was the approach of John's Gospel and of most classical theology: the Word was with God, and the Word became flesh.
- **Christology from below** begins with the historical Jesus of Nazareth—his life, teaching, death, and resurrection—and asks what must be said about him in the light of these. The German theologian **Wolfhart Pannenberg**, in *Jesus—God and Man* (1964), argued for this approach, grounding Christology in the history of Jesus and especially the resurrection.

The two approaches are complementary. The New Testament itself contains both: the Synoptic Gospels move from the man of Galilee toward the confession of his divinity, while John begins in eternity.

## The incarnation

The central Christian claim about Jesus is the **incarnation** (from the Latin *in carne*, "in the flesh"): that in Jesus of Nazareth, the eternal Son of God became a human being. John puts it simply: "the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us" (John 1:14). The claim is staggering. It means that the Creator entered his creation, that the infinite became finite, that the eternal entered time, that God was born as a baby, grew up in Nazareth, and died on a cross.

The doctrine of the incarnation was worked out in the fourth and fifth centuries, in a series of controversies that we follow in the next three pages. These controversies may seem abstract, but each concerned a question that the participants believed was vital for salvation: who must Christ be in order to save us?

## The virgin birth

Matthew and Luke both narrate that Jesus was conceived by the Holy Spirit and born of the Virgin Mary, without a human father, and the Apostles' Creed confesses it. Theologians have understood the **virginal conception** as a sign that the incarnation is God's initiative, a new beginning for humanity, not the product of human will. Some modern theologians have regarded it as symbolic rather than historical, noting that Mark, John, and Paul do not mention it. Most Christian traditions continue to affirm it as historical. It is important to distinguish it from the doctrine of the **Immaculate Conception**, a Catholic dogma defined in 1854, which concerns not Jesus's conception but Mary's: that she was preserved from original sin from the moment of her own conception.

> **Pause and consider.** Why might early Christians have believed that the question "Who is Jesus?" mattered for their salvation, and not only for their understanding?`,
