      title: "Arius and Nicaea",
      body: `## A priest in Alexandria

Around 318, **Arius**, a popular and learned priest in Alexandria, began to teach that the Son of God, though the first and greatest of God's creatures, through whom all else was made, was nonetheless a creature, not eternal. "There was [a time] when he was not," Arius's followers said. Only the Father was truly God, unbegotten and without beginning. The Son was begotten—that is, made—by the Father's will, before time, but not from eternity. He could be called "God" in a secondary sense, as a reward for his perfect obedience.

Arius's teaching had a logic. It preserved the absolute oneness and transcendence of God, the unbegotten Father, and it seemed to fit biblical texts such as "the Lord possessed me in the beginning of his way" (Proverbs 8:22, read as about the Son), "my Father is greater than I" (John 14:28), and "the firstborn of every creature" (Colossians 1:15). Arius set his teaching to popular songs, the *Thalia*, which were sung by sailors and dockworkers.

## Alexander and Athanasius

Arius's bishop, **Alexander** of Alexandria, condemned him. The dispute spread across the eastern empire. Alexander's deacon and secretary, a young man named **Athanasius** (c. 296–373), who would succeed him as bishop in 328, became the most determined opponent of Arianism. In his treatise *On the Incarnation*, written perhaps before the controversy, and in his later *Orations against the Arians*, Athanasius argued that only God can save. If the Son were a creature, he could not unite humanity with God, could not reveal the Father truly, and could not be rightly worshipped; to worship a creature is idolatry. "He became human that we might become divine," Athanasius wrote. Only one who is truly God can make us partakers of the divine nature.

## Constantine

The emperor **Constantine**, who had become a Christian patron after his victory at the Milvian Bridge in 312, was dismayed by the division in the church, which threatened the unity of the empire. In 325 he summoned a council of bishops to **Nicaea**, near his capital in Asia Minor. About 250–300 bishops attended, almost all from the East; tradition later fixed the number at 318. It was the first "**ecumenical**" council—a council of the whole church.

## The creed of Nicaea

The council produced a creed, built on an existing baptismal creed, with additions designed to exclude Arianism. It declared that the Son is:

> begotten of the Father, only-begotten, that is, of the substance of the Father, God of God, Light of Light, very God of very God, begotten, not made, of one substance with the Father.

The key word was ***homoousios***, "**of one substance**" or "of the same being" with the Father. It meant that the Son is fully divine, sharing the very being of the Father, not a lesser or created divinity. The creed concluded with anathemas condemning those who said "there was when he was not," or that the Son was "of another substance," or "created." Arius and two bishops who refused to sign were exiled.

## Why "homoousios"?

The word *homoousios* was controversial. It was not found in Scripture. It had been used by Gnostics and condemned at a council in Antioch in 268 in a different sense. Many bishops who rejected Arius were uneasy with it, fearing that it blurred the distinction between Father and Son. But the council chose it because the Arians could accept every biblical phrase by interpreting it in their own way. Only a non-biblical term could exclude their interpretation clearly. This was a turning point: the church used philosophical language to clarify the meaning of Scripture, a practice that has been both praised and criticized ever since.

## After Nicaea

Nicaea did not end the controversy. For more than fifty years, the church was divided. Emperors changed sides; Constantine himself later recalled Arius from exile, and was baptized on his deathbed by an Arian-leaning bishop. Athanasius was exiled five times by emperors who favored the Arians. Various compromise positions emerged: some said the Son was "**like**" the Father (*homoios*), others "**like in substance**" (*homoiousios*). The bishop **Jerome** wrote, of a council in 359 that adopted an ambiguous creed, that "the whole world groaned and was astonished to find itself Arian." The Gothic peoples, converted by Arian missionaries such as **Ulfilas**, who translated the Bible into Gothic, remained Arian for centuries.

Athanasius stood almost alone at times. A Latin phrase later summed up his stance: *Athanasius contra mundum*, "Athanasius against the world." His persistence, and the work of the theologians who followed him, finally secured the Nicene faith.

> **Pause and consider.** Why did the Council of Nicaea use a word not found in Scripture? Was this a betrayal of the Bible or a faithful interpretation of it?`,
