      title: "Constantinople and the Full Humanity of Christ",
      body: `## The Cappadocians

The Nicene faith was secured largely through the work of three theologians from Cappadocia, in central Asia Minor: **Basil of Caesarea** (c. 330–379), his younger brother **Gregory of Nyssa** (c. 335–395), and their friend **Gregory of Nazianzus** (c. 329–390). Known as the **Cappadocian Fathers**, they clarified the language of the Trinity, distinguishing between the one divine ***ousia*** (substance or being) and the three ***hypostases*** (persons): Father, Son, and Holy Spirit. One being, three persons: this formula, which we study in detail in SYS 201, became the standard expression of Trinitarian faith.

Their sister, **Macrina the Younger**, is honored as a saint and teacher in her own right; Gregory of Nyssa wrote her *Life* and a dialogue, *On the Soul and the Resurrection*, in which she is the teacher.

## The Council of Constantinople

In 381, the emperor **Theodosius I**, a firm Nicene, summoned a council to **Constantinople**. Gregory of Nazianzus presided for part of it, before resigning amid quarrels. The council reaffirmed the faith of Nicaea and condemned Arianism in its various forms. It also affirmed the full divinity of the **Holy Spirit**, against a group called the **Pneumatomachians** ("Spirit-fighters"), who regarded the Spirit as a creature. The creed associated with this council, an expansion of Nicaea's, is what is now usually called the **Nicene Creed** (more precisely, the Niceno-Constantinopolitan Creed). Its article on the Spirit confesses "the Lord, and Giver of life, who proceedeth from the Father, who with the Father and the Son together is worshipped and glorified, who spake by the Prophets."

## Apollinarius

The council also condemned another teaching, about the humanity of Christ. **Apollinarius** (c. 310–390), bishop of Laodicea, was a staunch defender of Nicaea and a friend of Athanasius. But he taught that in Christ, the divine Word took the place of the human mind or rational soul. Christ had a human body and an animal soul, but his mind was divine. Apollinarius thought this preserved the unity of Christ and his sinlessness: a human mind would be changeable and liable to sin.

## "What is not assumed is not healed"

**Gregory of Nazianzus** answered Apollinarius with one of the most famous principles of Christology:

> For that which he has not assumed he has not healed; but that which is united to his Godhead is also saved. If only half Adam fell, then that which Christ assumes and saves may be half also; but if the whole of his nature fell, it must be united to the whole nature of him that was begotten, and so be saved as a whole.
>
> — Gregory of Nazianzus, Letter 101, to Cledonius, trans. C. G. Browne and J. E. Swallow (1894)

The principle is soteriological: Christ saves human nature by taking it on, uniting it to God. If he did not take on a human mind, then the human mind—the very seat of sin—is not healed. Christ must be fully human, body and soul and mind, in order to save the whole human being.

## The full humanity

The Gospels portray Jesus's humanity vividly. He was born and grew "in wisdom and stature" (Luke 2:52). He was hungry and thirsty and tired. He wept at the tomb of Lazarus. He was angry at the moneychangers. He was "troubled" in spirit, and in Gethsemane his "sweat was as it were great drops of blood" (Luke 22:44). He asked questions, and said of the day of judgment that "of that day and that hour knoweth no man … neither the Son, but the Father" (Mark 13:32). Hebrews insists that he "was in all points tempted like as we are, yet without sin" (4:15).

The Council of Constantinople's rejection of Apollinarius affirmed that this humanity was complete. Christ is not God wearing a human body like a costume, nor a hybrid of divine and human, but fully human as well as fully divine.

## Docetism

Apollinarianism was a subtle version of an older error. In the earliest days of the church, some Christians, influenced by the belief that matter was evil, taught that Christ only **seemed** to have a body and to suffer. This view, called **docetism** (from Greek *dokein*, "to seem"), was opposed by the letters of John, as we saw in BIB 110, and by Ignatius of Antioch, who insisted that Christ "truly was born, ate, and drank, was truly persecuted under Pontius Pilate, was truly crucified and died." The church's defense of Christ's full humanity was also a defense of the goodness of the body and of the reality of salvation in history.

> **Pause and consider.** Gregory of Nazianzus argued that "what is not assumed is not healed." What does this principle imply about the extent of Christ's humanity? Could it be applied to other aspects of human life?`,
